# AI Server Patterns (apps/ai)

## Routes

Same structure as `crud` — one router per resource, `is_authenticated` first, export as `*_router`.

```ts
// src/routes/chat.ts
const router: IRouter = Router()
router.use(is_authenticated)
router.post("/:id/completion", chat_completion)
export { router as chat_router }
```

Mounted in `app.ts` at `/chats`, `/agents`, `/quiz`. Socket.IO is attached to the same HTTP server for mobile clients — same emitter pipeline.

## Controllers + SSE Transport

Controllers are thin — they set up SSE headers and delegate to a service function via **emitter callbacks**. This keeps the pipeline transport-agnostic (HTTP SSE and Socket.IO share the same service).

```ts
export const chat_completion = async (req: Request, res: Response) => {
  const { id: user_id } = req.user!
  const { id: conversation_id } = z_chat_completion_params.parse(req.params)
  const { prompt, user_files } = z_chat_completion_body.parse(req.body)

  await run_chat_completion({
    conversation_id, user_id, prompt, user_files,
    emitters: {
      on_validated: () => {
        res.setHeader("Content-Type", "text/event-stream")
        res.setHeader("Cache-Control", "no-cache")
        res.setHeader("Connection", "keep-alive")
        res.flushHeaders()
      },
      on_title:          (title) => res.write(`event: title\ndata: ${JSON.stringify({ title })}\n\n`),
      on_text_delta:     (text)  => res.write(`event: text-delta\ndata: ${JSON.stringify({ text })}\n\n`),
      on_thinking_delta: (text)  => res.write(`event: thinking-delta\ndata: ${JSON.stringify({ text })}\n\n`),
      on_tool_call:      (e)     => res.write(`event: tool-call\ndata: ${JSON.stringify(e)}\n\n`),
      on_tool_result:    (e)     => res.write(`event: tool-result\ndata: ${JSON.stringify(e)}\n\n`),
      on_status:         (text)  => res.write(`event: status\ndata: ${JSON.stringify({ text })}\n\n`),
      on_complete:       (id)    => { res.write(`event: complete\ndata: ${JSON.stringify({ conversation_id: id })}\n\n`); res.end() },
      on_error:          (msg)   => { res.write(`event: error\ndata: ${JSON.stringify({ message: msg })}\n\n`); res.end() },
    },
  })
}
```

**Rule:** Never write SSE headers inside a service — only in the controller emitter callbacks.

## Service Layer Pipeline

```
run_chat_completion
  └─ load conversation + agent from DB
  └─ persist user message
  └─ call on_validated() → SSE headers flushed
  └─ populate_tools(agent.tool_names, ctx)
  └─ run_agent_loop({ messages, agent, emitters, ... })
       └─ stream_text_with_fallback (Anthropic / OpenAI / Google)
            └─ for each tool_call → execute tool → yield tool-result
            └─ if tools were called → recurse loop with updated message history
       └─ persist assistant message to DB
       └─ call on_complete(conversation_id)
```

Services live in `src/services/`. Never put LLM logic in controllers.

## Tools — Definition & Execution

Each tool lives in its own folder under `src/tools/<tool-name>/`:
- `definition.ts` — Zod `inputSchema` + `description` (no execute logic)
- `execute.ts` — async execute function with full business logic

```ts
// src/tools/get-weather/definition.ts
export const get_weather_definition = {
  description: "Get the weather for a given location",
  inputSchema: z.object({
    location: z.string().describe("The location to get the weather for"),
  }),
}
```

```ts
// src/tools/get-weather/execute.ts
export const execute_get_weather = async (args: { location: string }) => {
  // fetch weather API, return structured result
  return { temperature: "...", condition: "..." }
}
```

**Registration** in `src/tools/index.ts` via `create_tools(ctx)` factory:

```ts
export const create_tools = (ctx: TToolContext) => ({
  ...static_tools,                          // tools with no context deps
  handoff_to_agent: tool({
    ...handoff_to_agent_definition,
    execute: (args) => execute_handoff_to_agent(args, ctx),
  }),
  get_ledger_file: tool({
    ...get_ledger_file_definition,
    execute: (args) => execute_get_ledger_file({ ...args, conversation: ctx.ledger_conversation_id }),
  }),
  // ...
})
```

**`populate_tools`** — resolves the agent's requested tool names + always includes `PLATFORM_TOOLS`:

```ts
export const populate_tools = (names: string[] | undefined, ctx: TToolContext): ToolSet => {
  const all_tools = create_tools(ctx)
  const tool_names = Array.from(new Set([...PLATFORM_TOOLS, ...(names ?? [])]))
  return Object.fromEntries(tool_names.filter(n => n in all_tools).map(n => [n, all_tools[n]]))
}
```

**Tool execution in the agent loop:**

```ts
for (const tc of tool_calls) {
  const tool = tools[tc.name]
  let result: unknown
  try {
    result = tool?.execute
      ? await tool.execute(tc.args, { toolCallId: tc.id, messages: [] })
      : { error: `Tool '${tc.name}' has no execute function` }
  } catch (err) {
    result = { error: err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err) }
  }
  yield { type: "tool-result", toolCallId: tc.id, toolName: tc.name, output: result }
}
```

Always wrap tool execution in try/catch and return `{ error }` — never throw from a tool into the stream.

## Agent Handoffs

The orchestrator calls the `handoff_to_agent` tool with an array of specialist targets. The tool:

1. Verifies each orchestrator → specialist relationship exists in `agent_handoffs` table
2. Creates a child conversation with `parent_conversation_id` + `root_conversation_id` pointers
3. Runs `run_agent_loop` for each specialist **in parallel** (`Promise.all`) with `is_specialist: true`
4. Specialists receive no SSE emitters — results are written to DB only
5. Returns `{ completed, failed, results, summary }` back to the orchestrator

```ts
// src/tools/handoff-to-agent/execute.ts
const results = await Promise.all(
  args.handoffs.map(async (handoff) => {
    // lazy import to break circular dependency
    const { run_agent_loop } = await import("@/services/agent-loop")
    const child = await create_child_conversation(...)
    await run_agent_loop({
      agent: specialist,
      conversation_id: child.id,
      ledger_conversation_id: root_conversation_id,
      callbacks: undefined,         // no SSE for child agents
      is_specialist: true,
    })
  })
)
```

**Getting handoff specialists:**

```ts
// src/services/agent-handoffs.ts
export const get_agent_handoff_specialists = async (agent_id: string) =>
  db.select({ id: agents.id, name: agents.name })
    .from(agent_handoffs)
    .innerJoin(agents, eq(agent_handoffs.specialist, agents.id))
    .where(eq(agent_handoffs.orchestrator, agent_id))
```

## Anthropic SDK Streaming

Uses the native `@anthropic-ai/sdk` streaming API and adapts events to Vercel AI SDK `TextStreamPart` shape:

```ts
const stream = client.messages.stream(request_params)
// map events: text-delta → text-delta, input_json_delta → tool-call, etc.
const final = await stream.finalMessage()
return { content: final.content, usage: { ... }, stop_reason: final.stop_reason }
```

- Convert tools via `convert_tools_to_anthropic()` (Zod → JSON Schema via `asSchema`)
- Convert messages via `convert_messages_to_anthropic()` before each call
- Multi-provider dispatch lives in `stream-text-with-fallback.ts` — add new providers there

## Auth (same pattern as crud)

`src/middleware/is_authenticated.ts` — identical to crud: Firebase token → DB user lookup → `req.user`.

`req.user` is typed via `src/types/express.d.ts`. Do not extend the middleware for AI-specific fields; load extra data in the service layer.

## Environment

Key env vars required in `src/constants/env.ts`:
- `DATABASE_URL` — Neon connection string
- `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`
- `FIREBASE_JSON_BASE64`
- `PORT` (default 8002 for ai)
